{"seconds": 123.5, "exit": 0, "final": "Black has no checks and no meaningful threats. White is just up a whole rook. The game is over. So the main line is:\n\n1. Rd5+ Kg6 (best try)\n2. Rxh4 and White is winning (up a full rook)\n\nActually, let me double-check if there's any defense after Rd5+. What if Black plays Kg4 (attacking the h8 rook)? Then Rxh4+ wins with check. What if Kf4? Then Rxh4+ also wins with check. So in all variations, White is winning immediately.\n\nThe key insight is that Rd5+ is a **fork** (or discovered attack) - it checks the king and makes the undefended rook on h4 fall. This is exactly the kind of tactical blow a club player should learn to recognize: when the king is exposed and the rook is loose, a check that exploits both can win material decisively.\n\n**Best move: Rd5+**\n\n**Main line: Rd5+ Kg6 Rxh4** (White wins the rook)\n\n**Motif: fork** (the rook checks the king while attacking the undefended enemy rook)\n\nNow let me write the coaching note:\n\n**Coaching Note:** When your opponent's king is exposed and a piece is undefended, look for forcing checks that hit both. Here, Rd5+ checks the king on g5 while the rook cannot defend itself on h4. After any king move, Rxh4 wins the rook outright. Exploit exposed kings and loose pieces\u2014they often go together.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Rd5+\", \"line\": [\"Rd5+\", \"Kg6\", \"Rxh4\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "7R/ppp2r2/5p2/6k1/7r/P7/1P6/1K1R4 w - - 2 32"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "7R/ppp2r2/5p2/6k1/7r/P7/1P6/1K1R4 w - - 2 32"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "7R/ppp2r2/5p2/6k1/7r/P7/1P6/1K1R4 w - - 2 32"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "7R/ppp2r2/5p2/6k1/7r/P7/1P6/1K1R4 w - - 2 32", "move": "Rg8+"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "7R/ppp2r2/5p2/6k1/7r/P7/1P6/1K1R4 w - - 2 32", "